WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:08.250
Whether it's revamping an old space

00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:11.430
or bringing a classic floor back to life,

00:00:11.430 --> 00:00:14.610
watch as three professionals
tackle age, neglect,

00:00:14.610 --> 00:00:16.650
and misuse that have produced

00:00:16.650 --> 00:00:20.220
some of the dirtiest and stickiest floors.

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:23.730
Kate Dear, the CEO of
Fêtewell, walks us through

00:00:23.730 --> 00:00:27.390
how she deep cleans a
bank floor made in 1908.

00:00:27.390 --> 00:00:29.944
It hasn't been touched in seven years,

00:00:29.944 --> 00:00:32.820
and she's revamping the
space into a wedding venue.

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:34.950
She found the tile under the carpet

00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:36.960
during an asbestos inspection

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:38.610
and decided to deep clean it

00:00:38.610 --> 00:00:40.950
so it could be part of the new space.

00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:44.280
The carpet is very thick
and therefore heavy,

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:45.570
so the first thing she does

00:00:45.570 --> 00:00:48.540
is get the carpet off
and let the tile breathe.

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:51.900
The carpet glue was the
thickest she'd ever encountered

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:54.540
and completely covered
the tile underneath.

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:56.808
The moisture and heat from the steam

00:00:56.808 --> 00:00:58.170
are some of the only things

00:00:58.170 --> 00:01:00.780
that would seep through
the glue to loosen it.

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:02.447
After she hits it with the steamer,

00:01:02.447 --> 00:01:05.340
she scrapes it up and starts
sanding the top layer,

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:07.890
where the glue stained the
tile with a yellow haze.

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:13.770
She adds Zep floor stripper,
which liquefies the glue

00:01:13.770 --> 00:01:15.933
so she's able to scrape
it up with a blade.

00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:20.673
When areas of the carpet glue

00:01:20.673 --> 00:01:22.364
become really tough to remove,

00:01:22.364 --> 00:01:24.210
she puts down a marble polish

00:01:24.210 --> 00:01:26.043
to help buff out those sections.

00:01:28.290 --> 00:01:32.085
The floor is old and uneven,
so her team needs to use

00:01:32.085 --> 00:01:35.003
a bristle-brush attachment
to get the steam in.

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:40.300
Then the team seals the tile
to protect it for the future.

00:01:52.173 --> 00:01:54.590
Kosta Ebanoidze, the owner of

00:01:54.590 --> 00:01:57.164
Pinnacle Stone Restoration,
walks us through

00:01:57.164 --> 00:01:59.880
how his team deep cleans terrazzo floors

00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:02.400
that are over 100 years old.

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:05.063
Parts of the terrazzo floors
were hidden under carpet

00:02:05.063 --> 00:02:07.383
and discovered as the
room was being renovated.

00:02:08.250 --> 00:02:10.050
The most difficult part of this clean

00:02:10.050 --> 00:02:12.425
is carpet and glue removal.

00:02:12.425 --> 00:02:15.240
He removes the carpets with
electric scraping machines

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:17.853
and the carpet glue
with handheld scrapers.

00:02:21.992 --> 00:02:24.042
Kosta removes the glue by hand

00:02:24.042 --> 00:02:25.680
because it keeps the temperature low,

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:27.210
which helps the glue come out more easily

00:02:27.210 --> 00:02:28.568
from the terrazzo.

00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:31.860
Grinding the terrazzo
floors helps to flatten

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:33.423
and even out the surface.

00:02:37.304 --> 00:02:39.750
Then he moves on to the honing process,

00:02:39.750 --> 00:02:41.460
which cleans and polishes off

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:44.627
the scratches left behind
by the grinding process.

00:02:44.627 --> 00:02:48.540
He starts polishing using
a 1,500-grit diamond pad,

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:50.790
followed by 3,000 grit.

00:02:50.790 --> 00:02:52.981
After that, he uses another system

00:02:52.981 --> 00:02:54.664
that has much finer pads,

00:02:54.664 --> 00:02:57.393
which make the terrazzo
harder and more durable.

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:01.500
Then it's time to seal.

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:04.487
Kosta uses a sealer that
seeps into the terrazzo

00:03:04.487 --> 00:03:06.930
and protects it from stains.

00:03:06.930 --> 00:03:08.665
Burnishing the floors helps the sealer

00:03:08.665 --> 00:03:11.043
bring the terrazzo to its highest sheen.

00:03:30.330 --> 00:03:31.710
Courtney Lee and his team

00:03:31.710 --> 00:03:33.840
at Truman's Hardwood Floor Refinishing

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:36.063
tackle dark, muddy-looking floors.

00:03:37.110 --> 00:03:38.970
They're covered with thick wax layers

00:03:38.970 --> 00:03:42.363
resulting from DIY attempts
at revitalizing wood floors.

00:03:43.410 --> 00:03:44.970
The acrylic waxes from products

00:03:44.970 --> 00:03:47.082
that are meant for short-term solutions

00:03:47.082 --> 00:03:51.060
are extremely soft and
don't harden up or cure.

00:03:51.060 --> 00:03:52.620
They sit on top of the floors

00:03:52.620 --> 00:03:55.825
instead of soaking in,
allowing dirt, soil,

00:03:55.825 --> 00:03:58.533
and any airborne
contaminants to bond to them.

00:03:58.533 --> 00:04:00.240
Courtney's team of floor technicians

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:03.183
specializes in wax removal
from hardwood floors.

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:07.419
Floor refinishing requires
sanding off existing coatings,

00:04:07.419 --> 00:04:09.037
but it's difficult to do

00:04:09.037 --> 00:04:11.360
with these layers of wax sitting on top

00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:14.555
because the wax will
likely clog up the sanders.

00:04:14.555 --> 00:04:16.710
The team comes in to emulsify the wax,

00:04:16.710 --> 00:04:18.900
which takes it back to a liquid form

00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:21.303
and allows them to extract
it from the floors.

00:04:22.590 --> 00:04:24.210
Buildup like this usually happens

00:04:24.210 --> 00:04:26.879
over the course of five to 20 years.

00:04:26.879 --> 00:04:29.452
They spray the floors with an emulsifier

00:04:29.452 --> 00:04:31.152
and let it sit for some time,

00:04:31.152 --> 00:04:33.635
depending on the severity
and amount of wax.

00:04:33.635 --> 00:04:35.694
They do this section by section.

00:04:35.694 --> 00:04:37.500
They follow up with extraction tools,

00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:40.920
which remove the wax from
the floors into the machine.

00:04:40.920 --> 00:04:43.051
Depending on how much wax there is,

00:04:43.051 --> 00:04:45.510
they repeat this two to three times.

00:04:45.510 --> 00:04:48.660
The buffer runs as a low
speed during this process.

00:04:48.660 --> 00:04:51.600
There is a soft pad and a
mesh screen on the bottom,

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:53.670
which comes in different grits.

00:04:53.670 --> 00:04:56.460
The screen scratches
off the old polyurethane

00:04:56.460 --> 00:04:59.460
so the team can apply new polyurethane.

00:04:59.460 --> 00:05:02.520
This is what seals and
protects the hardwood floors

00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:05.610
and is typically the final
layer before the wax.

00:05:05.610 --> 00:05:07.107
A splash guard is usually attached

00:05:07.107 --> 00:05:08.520
to the buffer to keep the wax

00:05:08.520 --> 00:05:11.103
from slinging onto the
walls and baseboards.

00:05:12.120 --> 00:05:13.800
In some cases, like here,

00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:16.500
Courtney will scrape off the wax by hand.

00:05:16.500 --> 00:05:19.440
He does this when there is
too much buildup on the floor.

00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:21.630
He'll scrape up as much as possible

00:05:21.630 --> 00:05:23.125
to prevent the wax from clogging up

00:05:23.125 --> 00:05:25.741
the vacuums in their machines

00:05:25.741 --> 00:05:28.266
and then remove the residual as normal.

00:05:29.850 --> 00:05:31.557
Once Courtney removes the wax,

00:05:31.557 --> 00:05:34.323
the floor is back to its
bare, original state.

00:05:35.460 --> 00:05:38.594
In some cases, depending on
what the homeowners want to do,

00:05:38.594 --> 00:05:42.394
he'll apply a new layer of
polyurethane to finish the job.

